{
  "dom_id": "step-q344825",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14215565/assets/3b68e9306032877d509bd8d0b38bac2dbf94ba4827ff501e200c51230442861b.png)\n\nFind the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x$ that is closest to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={(x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+\\frac{1}{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
